// examples/inventory/main.nika
//
// The same program `examples/report.nika` is, in **three files** - and the
// first thing in this repository that is more than one (Part I, 9.1).
//
// Input:  `category;item;count` per line
// Output: an HTML page written to the path given as the second argument
//
// Run:
//
//     nikaia --input examples/inventory/main.nika --backend rust
//     ./main stock.csv report.html
//
// `crates/nikaia/tests/examples.rs` compiles and runs it, and checks the page.
//
// Why it is here beside `report.nika` rather than instead of it. `report.nika`
// is the same program in one file, and having both is the point: a reader can
// see what a module boundary buys and what it costs, on a program small enough
// to hold in the head. What it buys here is that `stock.nika` can keep
// `weight` to itself, and that neither file has to be read to use the other.
//
// Three things about the multi-file build are worth knowing, and all three are
// the same answer - **it is name resolution, and Nikaia already had one place
// for that** (ADR-030).
//
//   * `stock::total(…)` is written the way it is *because* that is what it
//     lowers to. Every module becomes a `mod` at the crate root, so the
//     lowering stays name for name (ADR-011 D2) and nothing resolves anything.
//   * **One ledger for the project** (Part III, 13.5), keys qualified the way a
//     caller writes them - `stock::total`, `page::render` - which is the shape
//     `std.contracts` has had since ADR-020. So the type checker, the `sync`
//     check and the provenance analysis work across files without learning a
//     new trick.
//   * **`pub` is the whole of privacy.** `stock::weight` is not public, and
//     reaching it from here is `NK1110` before `rustc` is asked.

use stock
use page
use std::fs
use std::cli

fn main() throws {
    let path = cli::args().nth(1) ?? "stock.csv"
    let out = cli::args().nth(2) ?? "report.html"

    let data = fs::map(&path) catch {
        eprintln(f"cannot open {path}: {error}")
        return
    }

    let mut entries = stock::read(&data) catch {
        eprintln(f"{path}: {error}")
        return
    }

    entries.sort_by_key fn { -a.count }

    let total = stock::total(&entries)
    let count = entries.len()
    let markup = page::render(entries, total)

    fs::write(&out, &markup) catch {
        eprintln(f"cannot write {out}: {error}")
        return
    }

    println(f"{count} items, {total} in total")
}
